592186311648 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states the independence of the columns of {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ by the {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d9725c1-fcc5-57a9-8b5a-bd5efbda8207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311648 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L2}.$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states that the two columns of $Φ(t)$ are linearly independent, which is required of a fundamental matrix. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ states independence of the columns of $Φ(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the $\\text{properties of invertible matrices}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "511649c4-cd9c-5496-8df1-bb8892a20547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365659 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-6\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((6\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(6\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(6\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+6\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365660 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365660 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365660 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365660 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4302394f-a0de-5281-a556-4c43f2010e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365661 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}(6t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e09b9bd8-e653-5eb4-99a5-e24ca1275327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365663 #edb/ref 175921